solution:
from collections import Counter

def count_matching_pairs(items):
    counts = Counter(items)
    return sum(c * (c - 1) // 2 for c in counts.values())

A value that appears c times forms c choose 2 pairs, so the question reduces to counting. Counter makes one pass, O(n) time on average and O(k) extra memory for k distinct values; summing over the counts is O(k). It works for strings and lists because both are iterables of hashable items. An equivalent one-pass form adds `seen[x]` to the total before incrementing `seen[x]`, which also extends to 'pairs within a stream' without a second pass.

